In my spare time, I test generative AI by asking it to produce “academic” material in an ironic vein, often from minimal prompts. The purpose is playful, but it also offers a small experiment in what these systems can generate when academic language, methodological sophistication and plausibility are allowed to drift away from a meaningful research problem.

1. [Complex Models for Simple Data: A Complexity-First Framework for Unreasonably Sparse Evidence](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rxQ30mZXbvfZHfQa86lD5vgRJtZLtBDm/view)
2. [Stakeholder-Constrained Bayesian Updating: Prior Retrofitting and the Vanishing Data Principle](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Z_bQARmyk5vZyBzKdCaWZq8j0wFiFMEF/view)
3. [Half Full, Half Empty: Subjective Probability Beyond Extensional Coherence](https://drive.google.com/file/d/11UOBm_fK8WmghWulEuFV3I6jzk5Tf0kF/view)

These texts are AI-generated exercises and should not be interpreted as scientific contributions.
